ectly did a three-layer composition, cos^2(x^3), choosing over distractors for a dropped power-layer factor, a dropped inner factor, and the derivative plugged into the outer function. Before the shuffle he also got sin^3(2x), sin(x cos x) (chain rule plus product rule), and d/dx(y^3) = 3y^2 dy/dx (the implicit-differentiation setup). No ceiling found within single-variable computation. Don't reteach this.\"},{\"strand\":\"Evaluation point: f' at g(x)\",\"assessment\":\"Solid. With the answer position shuffled, he did the numeric table problem (f'(g(1))·g'(1)) and picked the conceptual reason: f only ever receives g(x) as input, so its local rate matters there. Earlier, with option-1 bias, he also got f(f(x)). He did not choose this as the part that feels unintuitive. Use it as a supporting idea, not a teaching target.\"},{\"strand\":\"Why the derivatives multiply (the target)\",\"assessment\":\"Floor: he recognizes the sequential-scaling explanation (chosen before the shuffle, so weak evidence). With position shuffled, he correctly estimated f(g(2.01)) by pushing a 0.01 nudge through each stage (×3, then ×4 → 0.12), rejecting the 'add the rates', 'ignore the inner stretch', and 'stop after g' distractors. So he can run nudge propagation on numbers. Ceiling: his own report is that it still doesn't *feel* intuitive. He can pick and use the explanation but doesn't own a picture of it. Recognition-level multiple choice can't measure this gap, so the teacher should have him generate explanations and predictions himself rather than select them. Build from: the nudge computation he just did, then generalize to 'a derivative is a local stretch factor, and stretches applied in sequence multiply'. Contrast with when adding is correct (two parallel paths, e.g. a product or"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":8,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":12131,"totalTokens":21601,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0