, not a teaching target."},{"strand":"Why the derivatives multiply (the target)","assessment":"Floor: he recognizes the sequential-scaling explanation (chosen before the shuffle, so weak evidence). With position shuffled, he correctly estimated f(g(2.01)) by pushing a 0.01 nudge through each stage (×3, then ×4 → 0.12), rejecting the 'add the rates', 'ignore the inner stretch', and 'stop after g' distractors. So he can run nudge propagation on numbers. Ceiling: his own report is that it still doesn't *feel* intuitive. He can pick and use the explanation but doesn't own a picture of it. Recognition-level multiple choice can't measure this gap, so the teacher should have him generate explanations and predictions himself rather than select them. Build from: the nudge computation he just did, then generalize to 'a derivative is a local stretch factor, and stretches applied in sequence multiply'. Contrast with when adding is correct (two parallel paths, e.g. a product or multivariable dependence) so multiplying feels forced rather than conventional. His thinking note 'inside first, then outside' may reflect the order a nudge flows through the layers (inner stage first)."}]}},"partialJson":"{\"assessment\": {\"summary\":\"Goal: make the chain rule feel intuitive, specifically why the derivatives multiply, rather than just applying it correctly. He has already shown the mechanics and the formal reasoning. The lesson should not teach procedure. It should start from the nudge-propagation picture he already handles numerically and turn it into a felt, visual model: local linearity / zooming in, stretch factors composing, and why adding is the wrong operation for a sequence of stages. Evidence caveat: every answer carried the same 70 confidence and the same thinking text ('inside first, then outside'). His reasoning was never visible, so the map rests on verdicts alone. The first 8 questions all had option 1 correct. The key ones were re-checked with the correct answer in other