, not a teaching target."},{"strand":"Why the derivatives multiply (the target)","assessment":"Floor: he recognizes the sequential-scaling explanation (chosen before the shuffle, so weak evidence). With position shuffled, he correctly estimated f(g(2.01)) by pushing a 0.01 nudge through each stage (×3, then ×4 → 0.12), rejecting the 'add the rates', 'ignore the inner stretch', and 'stop after g' distractors. So he can run nudge propagation on numbers. Ceiling: his own report is that it still doesn't *feel* intuitive. He can pick and use the explanation but doesn't own a picture of it. Recognition-level multiple choice can't measure this gap, so the teacher should have him generate explanations and predictions himself rather than select them. Build from: the nudge computation he just did, then generalize to 'a derivative is a local stretch factor, and stretches applied in sequence multiply'. Contrast with when adding is correct (two parallel paths, e.g. a product or multivariable dependence) so multiplying feels forced rather than conventional. His thinking note 'inside first, then outside' may reflect the order a nudge flows through the layers (inner stage first). That direction suits the propagation picture, but it runs opposite to the usual outside-in calculating procedure. Worth surfacing and reconciling the two directions."},{"strand":"Rigor / limit-based justification","assessment":"Ahead of the goal. He correctly identified the gap in the naive Δy/Δu·Δu/Δx proof (Δu = 0 arbitrarily close to x), first with the answer at option 1 and then, with the position shuffled, by picking x^2 sin(1/x) as the breaking case over x^3 (g'=0), |x|, and e^x. Not a target. At most, mention it briefly so the intuition isn't presented as the proof."},{"strand":"Supporting prerequisites (basic derivatives, product rule, linear approximation)","assessment":"Basic derivatives and the product rule hold (sin(x cos x) was correct). He used linear approximation f(a"}]}},"partialJson":"{\"