icitly and correctly in the nudge problem, but it was never probed as a named concept. The teacher can treat it as available and make it explicit as the foundation for the 'local stretch factor' picture."}]}},"partialJson":"{\"assessment\": {\"summary\":\"Goal: make the chain rule feel intuitive, specifically why the derivatives multiply, rather than just applying it correctly. He has already shown the mechanics and the formal reasoning. The lesson should not teach procedure. It should start from the nudge-propagation picture he already handles numerically and turn it into a felt, visual model: local linearity / zooming in, stretch factors composing, and why adding is the wrong operation for a sequence of stages. Evidence caveat: every answer carried the same 70 confidence and the same thinking text ('inside first, then outside'). His reasoning was never visible, so the map rests on verdicts alone. The first 8 questions all had option 1 correct. The key ones were re-checked with the correct answer in other positions, and he answered every re-check correctly.\",\"strands\":[{\"strand\":\"Chain rule mechanics (single variable)\",\"assessment\":\"Solid. With the correct option in a shuffled position, he correctly did a three-layer composition, cos^2(x^3), choosing over distractors for a dropped power-layer factor, a dropped inner factor, and the derivative plugged into the outer function. Before the shuffle he also got sin^3(2x), sin(x cos x) (chain rule plus product rule), and d/dx(y^3) = 3y^2 dy/dx (the implicit-differentiation setup). No ceiling found within single-variable computation. Don't reteach this.\"},{\"strand\":\"Evaluation point: f' at g(x)\",\"assessment\":\"Solid. With the answer position shuffled, he did the numeric table problem (f'(g(1))·g'(1)) and picked the conceptual reason: f only ever receives g(x) as input, so its local rate matters there. Earlier, with option-1 bias, he also got f(f(x)). He did not choose this as the part that feels unintuitive. Use it as a supporting idea, not a teaching target.\"},{\"strand\":\"Why the derivatives multiply (the target)\",\"assessment\":\"Floor: he recognizes the sequential-scaling explanation (chosen before the shuffle, so weak evidence). With position shuffled, he correctly estimated f(g(2.01)) by pushing a 0.01 nudge through each stage (×3, then ×4 → 0.12), rejecting the 'add the rates', 'ignore the inner stretch', and 'stop after g' distractors. So he can run nudge propagation on numbers. Ceiling: his own report is that it still doesn't *feel* intuitive. He can pick and use the explanation but doesn't own a picture of it. Recognition-level multiple choice can't measure this gap, so the teacher should have him generate explanations and predictions himself rather than select them. Build from: the nudge computation he just did, then generalize to 'a derivative is a local stretch factor, and stretches applied in sequence multiply'. Contrast with when adding is correct (two parallel paths, e.g. a product or multivariable dependence) so multiplying feels forced rather than conventional. His thinking note 'inside first, then outside' may reflect the order a nudge flows through the layers (inner stage first). That direction suits the propagation picture, but it runs opposite to the usual outside-in calculating procedure. Worth surfacing and reconciling the two directions.\"},{\"strand\":\"Rigor / limit-based justification\",\"assessment\":\"Ahead of the goal. He correctly identified the gap in the naive Δy/Δu·Δu/Δx proof (Δu = 0 arbitrarily close to x), first with the answer at option 1 and then, with the position shuffled, by picking x^2 sin(1/x) as the breaking case over x^3 (g'=0), |x|, and e^x. Not a target. At most, mention it briefly so the intuition isn't presented as the proof.\"},{\"strand\":\"Supporting prerequisites (basic derivatives, product rule, linear approximation)\",\"assessment\":\"Basic derivatives and the product rule hold (sin(x cos x) was correct). He used linear approximation f(a+h) ≈ f(a) + f'(a)h implicitly and correctly in the nudge problem, but it was never probed as a named concept. The teacher can treat it as available and make it explicit as the foundation for the 'local stretch factor' picture.\"}]}"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":8,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":12131,"totalTokens":21601,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":12131},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860897304,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQtJqpYAb2G7W2tPQwx"}}

